# Analysis and utility scripts
*.py
# But allow keyword-sync tools
!tools/keyword-sync/*.py
!tools/keyword-sync/output/
tools/keyword-sync/__pycache__/
# Everything directly under tools/ is a checked-in helper, not scratch. These are
# wired into validate-grammar.sh (orphan, duplicate, health and field-shape
# checks); if they are not tracked, a fresh clone silently skips those steps.
!tools/*.py
# Fault injectors for the validation gates (tools/gate-fixtures/README.md). A
# fixture that is not tracked is a fixture that does not survive, and these are
# the only proof the gates fail when they should.
!tools/gate-fixtures/*/*.py
# Written to the repo root by every text-format run of
# tools/find_unused_definitions.py — a report, not a source artifact
grammar_analysis.json
current_orphans.txt
orphan_report.txt
run_query*.sh
# .grammar_baseline.json is deliberately NOT listed here. Unlike the report
# above, it is a golden snapshot Step 8 of validate-grammar.sh (tools/check_grammar_health.py)
# diffs future runs against, so it is tracked in git on purpose: a baseline
# that only exists on one machine can't detect drift across commits, and a
# fresh clone needs it present or that step fails loudly by design (see
# CHANGELOG "Fixed" entry for validate-grammar.sh Step 8).
# Ignore queries/ except standard tree-sitter query files
queries/*
!queries/tags.scm
!queries/highlights.scm
!queries/locals.scm
!queries/indents.scm
!queries/folds.scm
!queries/textobjects.scm
FIXES.md

# AI assistant files
#
# `.claude/` holds BOTH local-only state (mind.mv2, settings.local.json, the
# per-machine commands) AND tracked project files: the release gate lives in
# .claude/commands/release.md and the grammar rules in .claude/rules/. A blanket
# `.claude/` ignore left those tracked subtrees invisible to `git status`, so a
# NEW rule file or a new gate command would be silently uncommittable — the same
# hazard that ate a corpus file via `property_*.txt`. Tracked files already in
# the index are unaffected by an ignore rule, which is why nobody noticed.
#
# git cannot re-include a path whose parent DIRECTORY is excluded, so this
# excludes the entries inside `.claude/` rather than `.claude/` itself, then
# re-includes the tracked subtrees. `rules/` and `skills/` are re-included
# wholesale because every file in them is project documentation and a new one
# should be visible; `commands/` is re-included by name because most of its
# contents are genuinely local-only.
#
# `skills/` was added after the rule above was written and inherited the
# blanket ignore, so `git add` refused a new SKILL.md with "ignored by one of
# your .gitignore files" — the same hazard the comment above describes, one
# directory over, caught only because the add was explicit rather than `-A`.
/.claude/*
!/.claude/rules/
!/.claude/skills/
!/.claude/commands/
/.claude/commands/*
!/.claude/commands/grammar-help.md
!/.claude/commands/release.md
!/.claude/commands/validate.md
.clinerules/
.goosehints
CLAUDE.md
settings.local.json

# Development and analysis files
*PLAN*.md
*SUMMARY*.md
*MIGRATION*.md
*MERGE*.md
*SOLUTION*.md
*CONVENTION*.md
*MISSING*.md
*QUERIES*.md
*RULES*.md
*GUIDE*.md
# Root-scoped ON PURPOSE. These are scratch dumps from the property-audit
# tooling, which writes them to the repo root. Without the leading slash the
# pattern matches at EVERY depth, and `property_*.txt` then silently swallowed
# test/corpus/property_comment_parameters_extended_test.txt — three real cases
# that ran for whoever had the file on disk and did not exist for anyone else.
# `git status` stays clean for ignored files, so the only symptom was a test
# total that disagreed between checkouts (1562 here vs 1559 in a fresh
# worktree). Keep new entries anchored unless you genuinely mean every depth.
/property_*.md
/property_*.txt
/all_properties.txt
/context_mapping.txt
/migration_strategy.md
/validation_report.md
/orphan_*.txt

